---
/**
 * `/editorial-policy/` — SPEC.md §14, published rather than internal.
 *
 * The red lines are stated here because they are also restated in the authoring
 * playbook: an agent will otherwise happily "complete" a section on any of
 * them, and a rule nobody can read is a rule nobody can hold us to.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../components/ui/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/blocks/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import Section from '../components/blocks/Section.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Editorial policy"
  description="What ARMAG will not publish, and why: no manufacturing instructions, no stopping-power metrics, no legal or regulatory layer."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-(--layout-max) px-(--layout-gutter) py-6">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Editorial policy' }]} />
    <h1 class="type-display mt-4 text-display text-ink">Editorial policy</h1>
    <p class="type-body mt-4 max-w-lede text-lg text-ink-secondary">
      These are permanent exclusions, not things awaiting a later version. Each one is enforced by
      the build where it can be, rather than by anyone remembering it.
    </p>

    <Section id="not-published" title="Not authored, not linked, not summarised">
      <ul class="prose type-body flex max-w-readable list-disc flex-col gap-2 pl-5 text-ink-secondary">
        <li>Manufacturing or fabrication instructions of any kind.</li>
        <li>Conversion to automatic fire, or any modification defeating a regulatory control.</li>
        <li>3D-printable files or their sources.</li>
        <li>Acquisition workarounds.</li>
        <li>
          Any content whose purpose is to help someone obtain or modify a firearm outside the
          ordinary lawful process.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="type-body mt-4 max-w-note text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Handloading data falls under the first line: there is no powder-charge field in the
        database, and there will not be one. Published velocity for a given barrel length is a
        measurement; a charge weight is a recipe.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Section id="no-stopping-power" title="No damage, lethality or stopping-power metric. Ever.">
      <div class="prose type-body max-w-readable text-ink-secondary">
        <p>
          Muzzle energy and retained energy are physics, and they appear prominently. Momentum and
          sectional density are physics too. <strong class="text-ink">Stopping power is not.</strong>
          It is a discredited idea with a long history of unsourceable numbers, and any index built
          on it — however scientific its formula looks — is a claim about wounding dressed as
          arithmetic.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          This includes indices that are conventionally published elsewhere. Taylor's Knock-Out
          factor is one: it was devised to rank dangerous-game rifles by their stunning effect, its
          dimensions are momentum times diameter, and it means nothing physically. It is not
          implemented here, and an automated test fails the build if it ever appears.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="no-legal-layer" title="No legal or regulatory layer">
      <div class="prose type-body max-w-readable text-ink-secondary">
        <p>
          No jurisdiction data, no legality flags, no filtering by what is permitted where.
          Technical facts — fire modes, barrel length, capacity — are recorded as facts, because
          that is what they are.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          This was considered and rejected rather than overlooked. Jurisdiction rules change yearly,
          and a stale legality flag on a static site is worse than none at all: it is wrong in a way
          that looks authoritative. The site states what a firearm is, never what you may do.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="no-winners" title="No winners">
      <div class="prose type-body max-w-readable text-ink-secondary">
        <p>
          Comparison tables carry no "winner" badges and the matchmaker ranks nothing on
          effectiveness. Lighter is better for carry and worse for recoil; higher capacity is better
          for some readers and irrelevant to others. The site does not know what you want, and
          pretending otherwise is exactly the editorial failure it exists to avoid.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          Where a bar is drawn next to a number, its length is that value's percentile within a
          stated comparison set, and the label is the real value with its real unit. Never a
          normalised score out of a hundred.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="imagery" title="Imagery">
      <div class="prose type-body max-w-readable text-ink-secondary">
        <p>
          Real photographs only. <strong class="text-ink">No AI-generated firearm imagery, ever.</strong>
          Image models get variant-level details wrong, which is the exact thing this site exists to
          get right, and a subtly incorrect photorealistic image does more damage to a reference work
          than a missing one.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          Every image is self-hosted and carries its licence, author and source in the credit panel
          on the image itself, not in a list somewhere else.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
